---
/**
 * Two complete sessions fetching one GitHub Actions failure log, rendered in full.
 *
 * WHAT YOU ARE LOOKING AT
 *
 * Every character of both sessions is on this page: the system prompt, every tool
 * definition, every call, every result, including the 11,232-token API response that
 * makes the argument. Nothing is truncated, nothing is elided, nothing says
 * "…truncated…". The panes scroll because the content is the point.
 *
 * Tool definitions are real — tools/list from @modelcontextprotocol/server-github, all
 * 26 of them. The API response bodies are synthesized to the live schema: same keys,
 * same nesting, same field sizes, measured against real responses from
 * fclidev/fcli/actions/runs (the three runs here cost 11,232 tokens against 12,573 for
 * the real three, an 11% difference that comes from commit message text). Log lines
 * follow GitHub's Actions format. The values are written, not captured.
 *
 * This is a synthetic session and says so. What is not synthetic: every number on the
 * page is o200k_base over the exact text below, computed by the same script that wrote
 * the text. There is no figure you can see here that was typed.
 *
 * WHY THE WHOLE THING RATHER THAN AN EXCERPT
 *
 * The argument is about volume. An excerpt demonstrates selection, which is exactly the
 * capability `f` lacks: `f gh GET` returns the whole body and there is no projection
 * flag, so the model reads all of it. Hiding the body to save pixels would remove the
 * evidence. It also makes the MCP pane honest — the server's 26 declarations are 3,575
 * tokens, and you can read every one of them to check that.
 *
 * WHAT THE COMPARISON SHOWS
 *
 * The runs endpoint sends `repository` and `head_repository` as the same 46-key object
 * twice, plus `head_commit` (2.5 kB) and two full user objects, to tell you eight
 * useful things. That is where the tokens go, on both sides. `f` spends 16,239 tokens
 * and MCP 20,461 on this job; the difference is declarations and index, 3,575+144
 * tokens times the turns, while the payload every one of those turns re-sends is shared.
 *
 * And the MCP server cannot do this job: tools/list has no workflow, job, log or
 * artifact tool, so the agent shells out through exec anyway. Scroll the declarations
 * and search for "workflow" — the only hit is a run URL inside a repository field.
 *
 * Regenerate: npm run measure:session
 */
import session from "../data/session-comparison.json";

const fmt = (v) => Number(v).toLocaleString();
const side = (s) => ({
  defs: s.metrics.defs,
  total: s.metrics.total,
  turns: s.turns,
  cum: s.metrics.cum,
  tools: s.tools,
  system: s.system,
});
const MCP = side(session.mcp);
const F = side(session.f);

// The visible-text total is the honest one: it is what the reader could sum by hand.
const diff = MCP.total - F.total;
---

<div class="compare">
  <p class="compare-job">
    Both sides, one job: find the failing Actions run, its failed job, and read the log.
    Full text, nothing truncated — the volume is the argument. Tool definitions are real
    (tools/list, 26 tools); API bodies are synthesized to the live schema. Every token
    figure below is <code>o200k_base</code> over the text you are reading.
  </p>

  <div class="compare-panes">
    {[{ key: "mcp", label: "MCP", sub: `${MCP.tools.length} tools + exec`, data: MCP }, { key: "f", label: "f", sub: "bash + jq + index", data: F }].map((p) => (
      <section class={"compare-col side-" + p.key} aria-label={`Session using ${p.label}`}>
        <p class="compare-head">
          <span class={"compare-svc" + (p.key === "f" ? " f" : "")}>{p.label}</span>
          <span class="compare-sub">{p.sub}</span>
          <span class="compare-count" data-count-for={p.key} data-count-to={p.data.total}><b>{fmt(p.data.total)}</b> tokens</span>
        </p>

        <div class="stream" data-stream={p.key}>
          <!-- The resident block: what every request pays before the model speaks. -->
          <div class="turn">
            <p class="turn-h">resident · system + {p.data.tools.length} tools <span>{" "}{fmt(p.data.defs)}</span></p>
            <pre class="ln body">{"system: " + p.data.system}</pre>
            {p.data.tools.map((t) => (
              <pre class="ln tooldef">{JSON.stringify(t, null, 1)}</pre>
            ))}
          </div>

          {p.data.turns.map((t, i) => (
            <div class="turn">
              <p class="turn-h">turn {i + 1} · cumulative <span>{" "}{fmt(p.data.cum[i])}</span></p>
              {t.user && <pre class="ln user">{"user: " + t.user}</pre>}
              {t.assistant && <pre class="ln asst-text">{t.assistant}</pre>}
              {t.call && <pre class="ln call">{JSON.stringify(t.call, null, 1)}</pre>}
              {t.result && <pre class="ln result">{t.result}</pre>}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="compare-verdict">
    Same job, same answer. <b>{fmt(MCP.total)}</b> against <b>{fmt(F.total)}</b> — the{" "}
    <b>{fmt(diff)}</b> difference is declarations and index, paid once per turn. What
    both pay, every turn, is the payload: {fmt(session.mcp.metrics.turns[1].result)}{" "}
    tokens for three runs whose useful content is eight fields, because{" "}
    <code>repository</code> and <code>head_repository</code> are the same 46-key object
    twice. <code>f gh</code> cannot project — no flag exists — so the model reads all of
    it; and the GitHub MCP server has <b>no Actions tool at all</b>, so it shells out
    through <code>exec</code> regardless. A uck is editable source: the projection is one
    file in this repo.{" "}
    <a href="/docs/measurements/">how these were measured</a>
  </p>
</div>
